# Keep Feature Docs In Sync

- Update the docs whenever feature behavior, API, data flow, config, UX, or operational behavior changes. No need to always do it, unless you are asked to do so, or you knew the docs file in current context of the conversation.
- Store feature documentation as markdown files under `docs/`.
- If the feature already has a docs file, update it, otherwise, create a new markdown file in `docs/` for that feature.
- Filename should start with a number and a underscore, e.g, `01_AUTH_LOGIN_FLOW.md`.
- In feature docs updates, include at minimum:
  - What changed (mandatory, keep it concise and to the point)
  - Why it changed (optional, keep it concise and to the point)
  - Rollout, migration, or backward-compatibility notes (if applicable)

# Types of documentation

1. Brand-new feature docs: This is a new feature that is being added to the system.
2. Optimization docs: This is an optimization to an existing feature.

# Format of the documentation

Note: `optional` means that the section is not mandatory, and can be skipped if not applicable.

## 1. Brand-new feature docs

Use following format:

```md
## Intro

(Describe the new feature in a few sentences.)

## Functional Requirements

## Non-Functional Requirements

## Use cases

## Possible Solutions

(Introduce the possible solutions for some use cases that are difficult/complex/important, no need for all use cases.)

### 1. How to implement [difficult/complex/important part here]?

#### 1.1. Solution A

- How it works
- Pros
- Cons
- Recommendation for our problem: Yes/No (optional)
- (If above is NO, then) When I’d use it (optional)

#### 1.2. Solution B

...

## High level Architecture/Design

### Component Diagram / Flowchart / Sequence Diagram

(Draw a component diagram, flowchart, or sequence diagram, to show how they interact with each other.)

### Core entities/models

List core entities/models and their relationships.

### API Draft

Usually should align with the use cases. Each use case should have a corresponding API draft. An use case may have multiple API drafts, or no API at all.

## Recommendation (optional)

(Just a recommendation path, do NOT implement anything in this section. Keep this short and concise.)

## Implementation details

(Implementation summary with phase numbers. After implementing a phase, update docs in this section, NOT modify the `Recommendation` section. In planning phase, do NOT write anything in this section.)

## Future Higher-Scale Path

## (Other sections you can add if needed)
```

## 2. Optimization docs

Use following format:

```md
## Current Problem

(Describe the current behavior or problem that we need to optimize/solve.)

## Possible Solutions

### 1. Solution A

- How it works
- Pros
- Cons
- Recommendation for our problem: Yes/No (optional)
- (If above is NO, then) When I’d use it (optional)

### 2. Solution B

## High level Architecture/Design (optional)

### Component Diagram / Flowchart / Sequence Diagram

(Draw a component diagram, flowchart, or sequence diagram, to show how they interact with each other.)

## Recommendation

(Just a recommendation path, or recommended solution, do NOT implement anything in this section. Keep this short and concise.)

## Implementation details

(Implementation summary for each phase, or the selected solution. After implementing, update docs in this section, NOT modify the `Recommendation` section. In planning phase, do NOT write anything in this section.)

## Future Higher-Scale Path

## (Other sections you can add if needed)
```
